    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Tue, Dec 6, 2016 at 5:08 PM, Jeff Alstott <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jeffa...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><span><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"auto\"><div><br></div><div>Are you using pystan? I believe it works fine in RStan. </div></div></blockquote></span><div>I am using pystan. </div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>Thanks for reporting back. For future reference, it helps to let us know up front that you&#39;re on pystan (or rstan or cmdstan).</div><div><br></div><div>The I/O is written separately for each of the interfaces, so errors in one interface may not translate to another interface.</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div><br></div><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><span><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"auto\"><div><br></div><div>When you say &quot;crash,&quot; what do you mean? Does it take down whatever you were running in? Or does it just quit?</div></div></blockquote></span><div>I upon running the model for sampling we get a runtime error, with the message I showed before:</div><div><pre style=\"box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;font-size:14px;line-height:inherit;word-break:break-all;word-wrap:break-word;color:rgb(0,0,0);border-radius:0px;white-space:pre-wrap;vertical-align:baseline\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:steelblue\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:darkred\">RuntimeError</span>: int variable contained non-int values; processing stage=data initialization; variable name=gap_lengths; base type=int</span></pre></div><div><br></div><div>Changing gap_lengths to an array of reals removes this message.  </div><div><br></div><div>In either case I&#39;m sending an empty list from Python. If I send an empty array, with type &#39;int&#39;, then we don&#39;t get this error. So it does seem to be in the plumbing from Python to Stan.</div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>That seems like the correct behavior. Remember that Stan is a strongly typed language. An array of reals is not an array of ints. It seems like an empty array in Python is created with a floating point type by default, so it&#39;s interpreted as an 0-length array of Stan type &quot;real.&quot; I&#39;m sure with clever enough I/O, someone could write better I/O in PyStan so empty arrays could be interpreted as both int arrays and real arrays, but the current behavior seems right for now.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Daniel</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
